WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Internal bleeding is one of the most challenging situations you might face because unlike a cut or a scrape, the danger is hidden beneath the skin.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Since you can't see the hemorrhage directly, you have to rely on your assessment of the situation to know when something is wrong.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:26.000
You should always suspect internal bleeding if someone has been involved in a high-impact event, such as a car accident, a significant fall, or a hard hit during a contact sport.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Because the bleeding is inside, the signs can be subtle at first.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
You're looking for things like bruising, shortness of breath, or complaints of intense pain in the chest or abdomen.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.000
If you feel the person's abdomen and the tissue feels unusually hard, or if they begin vomiting or coughing up blood, those are major red flags.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
You might also notice if the person is beginning to show signs of shock, such as appearing pale, cold, or confused.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.000
The most important thing to remember is that you cannot fix internal bleeding in the field.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
It requires a hospital and professional care.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Your priority is to call 911 immediately.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:13.000
While you wait for EMS to arrive, your secondary task is to control any external bleeding you can see to prevent further blood loss and keep the person as calm and still as possible.

